//? Title: The Iris Data: Summaries, Pictures and LDA
//? Subtitle: A short mepml report built on datamunge's C++ API
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A first look at Fisher's iris data with `datamunge`: we load the 150
flowers, summarize the four measurements overall and by species, draw a
pairs plot and per-species boxplots, and then fit a linear discriminant
analysis (LDA) with `stats::LDA`. Every table, number and figure below is the
output of a small C\+\+ program in this document, compiled against the
installed library when the report is run.
)

\toc

> The data

`dm::datasets::iris()` returns R's `datasets::iris`: 150 flowers,
50 each of ~setosa~, ~versicolor~ and ~virginica~, measured in centimetres
on sepal length and width and petal length and width. Each block in this
report is its own program, so each one loads the data again. Every block
starts with the single line `#include <dm/dm.hpp>`, which brings in the whole
library under the short namespace `dm` (and the standard headers a short
program needs). The first block just prints the size of the frame and its
first rows. `DataFrame::to_md()` writes a
frame as a Markdown table, like knitr's `kable()`: numbers are right-aligned,
and each numeric column gets the fewest decimals (at most three by default)
that show all of its values exactly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  std::print("The frame has {} rows and {} columns.\n\nHere's the first 6 rows of data: \n{}\n",
    iris.nrows(), iris.ncols(), iris.head(6).to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The frame has 150 rows and 5 columns.

Here's the first 6 rows of data: 
| Sepal.Length | Sepal.Width | Petal.Length | Petal.Width | Species |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 5.1 | 3.5 | 1.4 | 0.2 | setosa |
| 4.9 | 3.0 | 1.4 | 0.2 | setosa |
| 4.7 | 3.2 | 1.3 | 0.2 | setosa |
| 4.6 | 3.1 | 1.5 | 0.2 | setosa |
| 5.0 | 3.6 | 1.4 | 0.2 | setosa |
| 5.4 | 3.9 | 1.7 | 0.4 | setosa |
// result_end
\caption(The first six rows of the iris data)

> Summary statistics

The first table summarizes each measurement over all 150 flowers. It is a
dplyr-style pipeline: `pivot_longer()` stacks the four measurement columns
into one `cm` column with a `Measurement` column naming the source,
`group_by()` and `summarise()` then reduce each measurement to its mean,
standard deviation (with the \(n-1\) denominator), minimum, median and
maximum, and `to_md()` ends the pipeline with the table. The `dm::agg`
helpers name the aggregations as dplyr does, with the output column name
second.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  std::print("{}\n", 
    dm::datasets::iris() 
      | dm::pivot_longer({"Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width", "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"},
                         "Measurement", "cm")
      | dm::group_by("Measurement")
      | dm::summarise(agg::mean("cm", "Mean"), agg::sd("cm", "SD"), agg::min("cm", "Min"),
                      agg::median("cm", "Median"), agg::max("cm", "Max"))
      | dm::to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measurement | Mean | SD | Min | Median | Max |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Sepal.Length | 5.843 | 0.828 | 4.3 | 5.80 | 7.9 |
| Sepal.Width | 3.057 | 0.436 | 2.0 | 3.00 | 4.4 |
| Petal.Length | 3.758 | 1.765 | 1.0 | 4.35 | 6.9 |
| Petal.Width | 1.199 | 0.762 | 0.1 | 1.30 | 2.5 |
// result_end
\caption(Each measurement over all 150 flowers, in centimetres)

Grouping by species shows where the differences lie. `agg::n()` counts each
group's rows, and `agg::across()` applies one aggregation to several columns,
like dplyr's `summarise(n = n(), across(cols, mean))`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features = {
      "Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width", "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"};
  std::cout << (iris
      | dm::group_by("Species")
      | dm::summarise(agg::n(), agg::across(features, agg::Func::Mean))
      | dm::to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Species | n | Sepal.Length | Sepal.Width | Petal.Length | Petal.Width |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 5.006 | 3.428 | 1.462 | 0.246 |
| versicolor | 50 | 5.936 | 2.770 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 50 | 6.588 | 2.974 | 5.552 | 2.026 |
// result_end
\caption(Mean of each measurement by species, in centimetres)

The sepals of the three species overlap a good deal, but their petals do
not: ~setosa~'s petals are about a quarter the length of ~virginica~'s, and
~versicolor~ sits between the two.

> Pictures

A pairs plot (`graphics::Splom`, after R's `pairs()` and GGally's `ggpairs()`)
shows every pair of measurements at once. `group_by("Species")` splits every
panel by species. The diagonal shows each measurement's kernel density
estimate. Above it, each panel shows the flowers with a smooth estimate of the
mean (a local linear regression, like R's `loess()`) and a shaded 95%
prediction band, so the band shows where a new flower of that species would
fall. Below it, each panel shows contours of a two-dimensional kernel density
estimate, drawn at the levels that enclose 25%, 50%, 75% and 95% of each
species' flowers. The structure is plain: ~setosa~ is a cluster of its own in
every panel that involves a petal measurement, while ~versicolor~ and
~virginica~ touch.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/iris_splom.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  auto splom = dm::graphics::Splom::create(
      iris, {"Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width", "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", "Species"});
  splom.title("Iris measurements by species")
      .group_by("Species")
      .size(1040, 840);
  splom.save_svg("build/iris_splom.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/iris_splom.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Pairs plot of the four measurements: densities on the diagonal, smooths with 95% prediction bands above it, density contours below it; setosa in blue, versicolor in orange, virginica in red)
\alttext(
A four-by-four grid for the iris measurements. The diagonal shows one density
curve per species; the upper panels show the flowers with a smoothed mean line
and shaded prediction band per species; the lower panels show nested density
contours per species. Setosa forms a separate cluster in the petal panels
while versicolor and virginica overlap slightly.
)

Boxplots put the same information one measurement at a time. Petal length
and width separate all three species with little overlap; sepal width is
the only measurement in which ~setosa~ is the largest.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/iris_boxplots.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  using ColumnType = dm::dstruct::DataFrame::ColumnType;
  const std::vector<std::string> species = dm::unique(iris.get_column<std::string>("Species"));
  const std::vector<std::string> features = dm::col_names(iris, ColumnType::Numeric);
  // The pairs plot's colours: blue, orange and red.
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors = {{78, 121, 167}, {242, 142, 43}, {225, 87, 89}};

  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2).size(960, 720);
  for (const auto& f : features) {
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> groups;
    for (const auto& sp : species) {
      const auto subset = iris.filter([&](const auto& r) {
        return r.get_string("Species") == sp;
      });
      groups.push_back(subset.numeric_column(f).values());
    }
    auto bp = dm::Plot::boxplot(groups, species, colors);
    bp.title(f).x_label("").y_label("cm").size(480, 360);
    layout.add(bp);
  }
  layout.save_svg("build/iris_boxplots.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/iris_boxplots.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Each measurement by species)
\alttext(
Four boxplot panels, one per measurement, each with a box for setosa,
versicolor and virginica; the petal panels show three well separated boxes.
)

> Linear discriminant analysis

LDA models each species as a multivariate normal distribution with its own
mean \(\mu_k\) and a covariance matrix \(\Sigma\) shared by all three. Under
that model the most probable species for a flower \(x\) is the \(k\) that
maximizes the linear discriminant function

$$
\delta_k(x) = x^\top \Sigma^{-1}\mu_k - \tfrac12\,\mu_k^\top \Sigma^{-1}\mu_k + \log \pi_k ,
$$
\alttext(delta k of x equals x transpose Sigma inverse mu k, minus one half
mu k transpose Sigma inverse mu k, plus log pi k)

where \(\pi_k\) is the prior probability of species \(k\). The same model
also gives Fisher's discriminant axes: the directions \(w\) that maximize the
ratio of between-species to within-species variance. With three species
there are at most two such axes, LD1 and LD2.

>> Fitting the model

`stats::LDA` takes a data frame and an R-style formula. `to_md()` reports the
fit as Markdown tables with the same content as R's `MASS::lda()` printout:
the priors, the group means, the coefficients of each discriminant axis, and
the proportion of trace, which says how much of the separation each axis
carries. (`print_summary()` gives the same report as plain text.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::print("{}\n", dm::stats::LDA(dm::datasets::iris(), 
      "Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width").to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Linear discriminant analysis of `Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width` on 150 observations and 3 classes; training accuracy 98.00%.

Prior probabilities of groups:

| setosa | versicolor | virginica |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.3333 | 0.3333 | 0.3333 |

Group means:

| class | Sepal.Length | Sepal.Width | Petal.Length | Petal.Width |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 5.006 | 3.428 | 1.462 | 0.246 |
| versicolor | 5.936 | 2.770 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 6.588 | 2.974 | 5.552 | 2.026 |

Coefficients of linear discriminants:

| predictor | LD1 | LD2 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Sepal.Length | -0.8294 | 0.0241 |
| Sepal.Width | -1.5345 | 2.1645 |
| Petal.Length | 2.2012 | -0.9319 |
| Petal.Width | 2.8105 | 2.8392 |

Proportion of trace:

| LD1 | LD2 |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0.9912 | 0.0088 |
// result_end

LD1 carries 99% of the separation (its proportion of trace). Its largest
coefficients are on the petal measurements, which matches what the pictures
above showed.

>> How well it classifies

The confusion matrix compares each flower's species with the one the fitted
model predicts for it (rows are the true species, columns the predicted
ones). `confusion_frame()` returns it as a data frame, so it pipes into
`rename()` and `to_md()` like any other.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const dm::stats::LDA model(dm::datasets::iris(),
    "Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width");
  std::print("Training accuracy: {:.1f}%\n\n{}\n",
     100.0 * model.training_accuracy(),
     (model.confusion_frame() | dm::rename("actual", "True species") | dm::to_md()));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Training accuracy: 98.0%

| True species | setosa | versicolor | virginica |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 48 | 2 |
| virginica | 0 | 1 | 49 |
// result_end
\caption(Confusion matrix of the fitted model on its own training data)

Accuracy measured on the flowers the model was fitted to is optimistic. A
fairer estimate is five-fold cross-validation: each flower goes into one of
five folds (every fifth row, so each fold holds ten flowers of each
species), the model is fitted to the other four folds, and the held-out fold
is predicted.

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  constexpr int k = 5;
  std::vector<double> fold(iris.nrows());
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fold.size(); ++i) fold[i] = static_cast<double>(i % k);
  iris.add_column("fold", fold);

  std::size_t correct = 0, total = 0;
  // Loop through folds and update :
  for (int f = 0; f < k; ++f) {
    const auto train = iris.filter(
        [&](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("fold") != f; });
    const auto test = iris.filter(
        [&](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("fold") == f; });
    const dm::stats::LDA model(train,
      "Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width");
    const auto predicted = model.predict(test);
    const auto& actual = test.string_column("Species").values();
    std::size_t hits = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < predicted.size(); ++i) hits += predicted[i] == actual[i];
    std::print("fold {}: {:2} / {} correct\n", f + 1, hits, predicted.size());
    correct += hits;
    total += predicted.size();
  }
  std::print("\ncross-validated accuracy: {:.1f}% ({} / {})\n",
    100.0 * static_cast<double>(correct) / total, correct, total);
}
```
// result_begin:
// fold 1: 29 / 30 correct
// fold 2: 30 / 30 correct
// fold 3: 30 / 30 correct
// fold 4: 28 / 30 correct
// fold 5: 30 / 30 correct
//
// cross-validated accuracy: 98.0% (147 / 150)
// result_end

>> The discriminant space

Projecting every flower onto LD1 and LD2 turns the fitted model into a
picture. `dm::plot()` draws an object's default picture and returns a
`graphics::Figure`, which is titled, sized and saved the same way whatever
is in it (`save_svg()`, `save_png()`, `save_pdf()`, or `to_svg()` for the
text). For an LDA fit it draws that projection, coloured by the true
species, over the model's decision regions: each shaded area is where
the model predicts that species, and the dark lines are the boundaries
between them. In discriminant space LDA assigns a flower to the species whose
mean is nearest (after a small adjustment for the priors), so the boundaries
are straight lines and each region is a convex polygon. With three species
the two axes carry the whole model, so these regions are exactly the model's
predictions.

The three groups line up along LD1, with ~setosa~ far from the other two.
The flowers on the wrong side of the ~versicolor~ / ~virginica~ boundary are
the three misclassified in the confusion matrix.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/iris_lda_discriminants.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::stats::LDA model(
      dm::datasets::iris(), "Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width");
  dm::plot(model).size(840, 560).save_svg("build/iris_lda_discriminants.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/iris_lda_discriminants.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The iris flowers on the two discriminant axes, coloured by species, over the model's decision regions)
\alttext(
A scatter plot of LD1 against LD2 with three clusters of points on a
background split into three shaded regions by two straight boundary lines;
setosa is far to one side and versicolor and virginica sit next to each
other, with three flowers on the wrong side of the line between them.
)

>> Boundaries on the petals

Decision regions can also be drawn in the measurements themselves when the
model uses two of them. The petals carry most of the separation, so a model
with only petal length and width is a natural choice. For a model with two
predictors `dm::plot()` gives two panels: the discriminant space as before,
and the predictor space, shaded by the species the model predicts at each
point. The boundaries are straight lines in both, because LDA's discriminant
functions are linear in the measurements.

```{cpp, results="output", file="figures/iris_lda_petal_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::stats::LDA petals(dm::datasets::iris(), "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width");
  dm::plot(petals)
      .title("LDA on petal length and width")
      .size(1200, 520)
      .save_svg("build/iris_lda_petal_regions.svg");
  std::print("training accuracy: {:.1f}%\n", 100.0 * petals.training_accuracy());
}
```
// result_begin:
// training accuracy: 96.0%
// \image(figures/iris_lda_petal_regions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(An LDA on petal length and width: the flowers in discriminant space (left) and in the petal plane (right), each over the model's decision regions)
\alttext(
Two panels. Left: the flowers on LD1 and LD2 over three shaded regions.
Right: petal width against petal length on a background split into three
shaded regions by two straight lines: setosa's small petals sit alone in the
lower left, versicolor in the middle and virginica in the upper right, with a
few flowers near the versicolor-virginica line.
)

The two petal measurements alone classify 96% of the training flowers
correctly, against 98% for all four; the six errors all sit near the line
between ~versicolor~ and ~virginica~.

> Conclusion

The four measurements separate ~setosa~ from the other species completely, and
the petal measurements do most of the work. LDA confirms this: one discriminant
axis carries almost all of the separation, the fitted model misclassifies only
three ~versicolor~ and ~virginica~ flowers, and five-fold cross-validation gives
the same 98% accuracy, so the fit is not an artefact of testing on the training
data. A model on the petals alone does nearly as well, and its decision regions
show why: two straight lines in the petal plane separate the species almost
perfectly. The longer report in `examples/mepml/lda_iris_analysis.mepml` derives
LDA in detail and predicts new flowers.
